# Accepted Trivy findings.
#
# Each entry carries why it is accepted and when it must be looked at again.
# An entry without both is not allowed, and neither is adding one to make a
# build green: these are risks the project has decided to run, not checks it
# has switched off. Anything not listed here fails the build.
#
# Reviewed: 9 September 2026.

# AWS-0040 — EKS public endpoint enabled.
# The endpoint is public but restricted: modules/cluster rejects 0.0.0.0/0 with
# a validation block, so a caller must name explicit CIDRs. A fully private
# endpoint needs a VPN or a bastion reachable from the team's networks, which
# the credit budget does not cover and which ADR-006 deliberately avoided by
# using SSM Session Manager. Authentication is IAM, so the endpoint being
# reachable is not the same as it being open.
# Revisit if the project gains a VPN, or before any real client data lands.
AWS-0040 exp:2026-12-31

# AWS-0104 (x2) — unrestricted egress on the ALB and node security groups.
# Documented in modules/namespace/README.md and in the architecture: closing
# egress breaks name resolution, image pulls from ECR, the SSM calls External
# Secrets depends on, and the STS request behind IRSA. Ingress is what carries
# the isolation, because it is the destination that refuses the connection.
# Revisit if VPC endpoints are added for ECR, STS and SSM, which is what would
# make a restricted egress list workable.
AWS-0104 exp:2026-12-31

# AWS-0164 — public subnets auto-assign a public IP.
# Nothing is launched into them: the nodes live in the private subnets, and the
# ALB and NAT Gateway take their addresses from AWS rather than from this
# attribute. Turning it off is very likely safe and is worth doing, but it is a
# change to live network configuration that deserves its own pull request and
# its own plan rather than riding along with a CI fix.
# Revisit: next infrastructure change touching modules/network.
AWS-0164 exp:2026-10-31

# AWS-0039 — EKS secret envelope encryption not enabled.
# This one is a genuine gap rather than a decided trade-off, and it is the one
# on this list worth acting on. Mitigating it today: real secrets live in SSM
# Parameter Store outside the cluster (ADR-002), and the cluster is destroyed
# and recreated routinely (ADR-010), so what sits in etcd is short-lived.
# Enabling it needs a KMS key, is a one-way change on an existing cluster, and
# costs about 1 USD a month.
# Revisit: before any real client data is handled. Tracked for card #19.
AWS-0039 exp:2026-10-31
